{"gid":"2026_01_NO_DET","week":1,"away":"NO","home":"DET","away_col":"#D9BE72","home_col":"#4FA8E8","spread":7.0,"total":48.5,"fav":"DET","hpts":28,"apts":21,"kickoff":"Sun Sep 13 \u00b7 13:00","roof":"Dome","report_html":"<div class=\"report\"><p><strong>Script leans DET.</strong> As a 7-point favorite they project to lead much of the game, tilting toward the run late \u2014 <strong>Jahmyr Gibbs</strong>'s carry ceiling rises in the blowout branches. Meanwhile NO throw to keep pace, a volume tailwind for <strong>Chris Olave</strong>.</p><p>The <strong>DET</strong> host the <strong>NO</strong> as favorites of <strong>7.0</strong>, with the total set at <strong>48.5</strong> \u2014 a market that implies a <strong>DET 28\u201321</strong> scoreline. Running the game forward from that baseline, we expect a contest that spends roughly <strong>38% of its snaps with DET in front</strong>, <strong>38% within one score</strong>, and <strong>24% with DET chasing</strong>. That distribution is the engine of everything below: game state is what turns a team's tendencies into a specific player's workload, so before we get to individuals it's worth being explicit about <em>how</em> this game is likely to be played.</p><p>This projects as a game <strong>DET should control</strong>. A favorite of 7 sits in front for the majority of the second half in most simulations, which pulls the script toward the run and clock management for <strong>DET</strong> and toward volume passing and tempo for <strong>NO</strong>, who will be throwing to keep pace. Expect <strong>NO</strong> to run more total plays than DET \u2014 trailing teams go no-huddle (league no-huddle rate climbs from ~8% in neutral game states to over 20% when down two scores), and that extra volume is a tailwind for NO's pass-catchers even in a losing effort.</p><h3>The Key Matchups</h3><p>Tough sledding for <strong>Travis Etienne</strong> \u2014 DET are stingy against the run (109 yds/game, well below the ~120 norm); lean under on his rushing line.</p><p><strong>Tyler Shough</strong> has a passing-yards tailwind \u2014 DET allow <strong>249 pass yds/game</strong> (vs ~230).</p><p>Sneaky value on <strong>Devaughn Vele</strong> (WR2) \u2014 DET leak 45 yds/game to the WR2 role, above the ~36 norm.</p><p><strong>Amon-Ra St. Brown</strong> faces a tough cover \u2014 NO hold WR1s to just 51 yds/game (vs ~58); a rec-yards under lean.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Juwan Johnson</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 DET are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.29 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><p><strong>Amon-Ra St. Brown</strong> carries TD equity \u2014 NO surrender 0.47 WR1 receiving scores a game.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p><strong>Indoors</strong> \u2014 a dome removes weather variance and historically nudges passing efficiency up; a small tailwind for the passing games.</p><h3>DET \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>DET are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (42% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p><strong>Jahmyr Gibbs</strong> (14 carries, 73 yards projected) gets a mild game-script tailwind as a favorite, though DET's willingness to keep throwing when ahead caps how much the lead converts into extra carries.</p><p><strong>Amon-Ra St. Brown</strong> projects for <strong>9 targets and 79 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Sam LaPorta</strong> (TE, 6 targets, 47 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>NO \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>NO are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>37%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 NO project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Travis Etienne</strong>'s rushing projection (13 carries, 57 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Chris Olave</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, NO funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>9 targets for 75 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Juwan Johnson</strong> (TE, 5 targets, 38 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 7-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather and line moves update the inputs.</em></p></div>","teams":[{"team":"NO","col":"#D9BE72","players":[{"name":"Tyler Shough","pos":"QB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Pass yards","proj":231.64,"book":246.5,"over_th":217.81,"under_th":243.15,"over_am":"\u2212114",